# PeckNSend

PeckNSend is a local multiplayer Unity game prototype where players control birds, collect mail, 
and deliver it to matching mailboxes for points. The project was built as a team game project, 
with my work focused on scene flow, UI/game-state architecture, player session management, match scoring, and development tooling.

## My Contributions

My main contributions are in:

- `FSM/`
- `Models/`
- `Presenters/`
- `Debugging/`
- `Singleton/`

I did NOT create the code in:

- `Background/`
- `Mail/`
- `MailboxLogic/`
- `Player/`
- `Scripts/` (the loose scripts)

## What I Built

I implemented the core application flow using finite state machines and a model/presenter structure. 
The goal was to keep scene transitions, UI state, and match state organized instead of placing all logic directly inside Unity UI scripts.

Key systems I worked on include:

- Scene flow between boot, main menu, player join, play, and result scenes
- Main menu screen state management
- Player join and ready-up tracking
- Persistent game session data
- Match statistics and score reporting
- Result screen presentation
- Direct-entry scene bootstrappers for faster Unity playtesting

## Notable Systems

### Scene Flow System

- `Presenters/SceneSwitchSystem/SceneManagerPresenter.cs`
- `Models/SceneSwithSystem/SceneManagerModel.cs`
- `Models/SceneSwithSystem/SceneManagerFSM.cs`
- `Models/SceneSwithSystem/SceneBaseState.cs`

This system controls the main flow between boot, main menu, player join, play, and result scenes. 
I used a finite state machine so scene transitions are requested through clear state-specific methods instead of being triggered directly from scattered UI scripts.

### Persistent Game Session System

- `Presenters/GameSessionSystem/GameSessionPresenter.cs`
- `Models/GameSessionSystem/GameSessionModel.cs`
- `Models/PlayerJoinScene/JoinedPlayerData.cs`
- `Models/PlayScene/MatchStatsModel.cs`
- `Models/PlayScene/PlayerMatchStatsModel.cs`

This system stores player and match data across scene changes. It keeps track of joined players, 
controller/device information, bird selections, and delivered mail scores so gameplay and result scenes can share the same session data.

### Direct Scene Testing Tools

- `Debugging/MainMenuSceneDirectEntryBootstrapper.cs`
- `Debugging/PlayerJoinSceneDirectEntryBootstrapper.cs`
- `Debugging/PlaySceneDirectEntryBootstrapper.cs`
- `Debugging/ResultSceneDirectEntryBootstrapper.cs`

These bootstrappers make individual Unity scenes easier to test by creating required persistent services and debug session data when entering a scene directly.

## Current Status

This repository is a portfolio/code sample from a Unity game project, not a polished production release. 
Some features, such as pause handling, were still unfinished at the time this code was shared.

## What I Learned

This project helped me practice:

- Structuring Unity code beyond single-purpose MonoBehaviours
- Separating UI presentation from game state
- Managing persistent data across scenes
- Using finite state machines for menus and game flow
- Supporting multiplayer player join and ready-up workflows
- Building development tools to speed up testing

## Future Improvements

If I continued development, I would focus on:

- Finishing or removing incomplete pause behavior
- Adding more validation around Unity inspector references
- Improving null-safety around scene and singleton dependencies
- Renaming/refactoring older folders and comments for polish
- Adding automated tests for state transitions, ready-up logic, and scoring
- Documenting scene setup and required Unity packages
